source: src/dispatch/prompts_snapshots.rs
expression: cve_prompt(true)
---
Your task is:
Task:
  ID: 42
  Title: [HIGH] dispatch: CVE-2026-1234
  Description: idna accepts Punycode labels that do not produce any non-ASCII output

Fix PR: https://github.com/o/r/pull/7 (Bump idna to 3.7), matched by advisory id
  EpicId: 9

This task is part of epic #9: CVE
If you need help, or a sibling task's agent is working on a similar problem, ask that agent.

This is a CVE remediation task. It is a targeted dependency fix, not a feature.
Do not design it spec-first, and do not write a failing test first — neither
matches the shape of this work.

Start from the advisory. The description carries the summary; the task's URL
points at the alert. Identify the vulnerable package, the affected range, and
the exact versions the advisory lists as fixed. Only a version in that fixed set
counts: a higher version number is not automatically patched, because fixes are
backported to maintenance branches and a newer minor released earlier can still
be vulnerable.

The description's `Fix PR:` line names an open PR in this repo that already
fixes the alert. Review and merge that PR instead of writing your own fix.

1. Confirm the PR lands on a version in the fixed set (gh pr diff <PR>). The
   line ends with how the feed matched it; a package-name match proves least.
2. Confirm a dependency bot opened it and that it changes only dependency
   manifests and lockfiles (gh pr view <PR> --json author,files,headRefOid).
   Note headRefOid: it is the commit you are reviewing.
3. Check CI: gh pr checks <PR>. It must pass.
4. Check Kognic's GitHub App verdict: gh pr view <PR> --json reviews,comments.
   The app is `kognic-github-app`. Its latest review must be APPROVED.
5. If all of these hold: gh pr review <PR> --approve, with a body saying what
   you checked, then
   gh pr merge <PR> --squash --auto --match-head-commit <headRefOid>, so a push
   after your review cannot be merged unseen. Wrap up on the path with
   no git operations. The feed removes this task once the alert closes.
6. If any check fails, tell the user what you found and stop. Do not write a
   second fix that competes with the PR.

Knowledge base: the `/learnings` skill manages it — check it when something is unclear, and record what you find.

When your work is done — finishing implementation, or (for an epic-decomposition task) creating work packages for its subtasks, and not before — use the dispatch:wrap-up skill to commit any remaining changes and finalise the task.
